00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:31.150
So we're talking about
Ruby, the language.

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:33.190
I guess then why Ruby?

00:04:33.190 --> 00:04:34.850
Why are we talking about this?

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:37.517
AJA HAMMERLY: So the Ruby
community is surprisingly big.

00:04:37.517 --> 00:04:39.350
A lot of people don't
realize how big it is.

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:40.690
It's being taught in
a lot of boot camps,

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:42.430
and there's a lot of
relatively large websites

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:43.263
that are running it.

00:04:43.263 --> 00:04:45.730
I think my favorite example
is GitHub, which most of us

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:46.740
use nearly every day.

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:48.670
It's got a fair amount
of Ruby internally.

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:49.420
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:51.439
AJA HAMMERLY: And
it's a huge market.

00:04:51.439 --> 00:04:52.480
There was a lot of stuff.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:54.970
And we were trying to figure
out as part of Cloud Platform,

00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:56.450
how can we support
our developers better?

00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:58.270
And one of those ways is we
can support the languages

00:04:58.270 --> 00:05:00.269
they're using and meeting
them where they're at.

00:05:00.269 --> 00:05:02.069
And so I've been part
of our Cloud Ruby

00:05:02.069 --> 00:05:03.610
effort over the last
couple of months

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:05.950
as we're trying to
beef up our libraries,

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:08.020
beef up our support for
Ruby, and make it a place

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:09.750
that Rubyists feel comfortable.

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:10.090
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:11.290
So why don't you
tell us a little bit

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:13.670
about what kind of things
that you've been working on.

00:05:13.670 --> 00:05:15.669
AJA HAMMERLY: So, our two
big projects right now

00:05:15.669 --> 00:05:18.040
are we're writing our
Google Cloud libraries,

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:20.470
being released as Ruby
Gems, and more and more

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:23.762
coming out nearly every
day, on a regular basis.

00:05:23.762 --> 00:05:25.220
All this is being
done in the open.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:28.660
It's all on GitHub under our
Google Cloud Platform Project.

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:30.700
And the other thing
we've been working on

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:33.520
is Ruby support in Rails
support for App Engine Flex.

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:35.560
We also support Sinatra,
the other big web

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:37.000
framework for Ruby.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:38.920
And I'm really happy
with where that's at.

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:42.670
I actually got to participate in
a hackathon with it last week.

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:45.340
And I played a couple of my
personal projects, my side

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:47.410
projects, to the platform.

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:48.260
And it was awesome.

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:50.500
It was great that they
came up real quickly.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:52.390
I got to see
everything in place.

00:05:52.390 --> 00:05:55.030
Hooked it into Cloud SQL
with no issue at all,

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:58.360
just trying to get the right
IP address and the right files.

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:00.550
And it was really
awesome having something

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:04.050
that was that seamless and
that easy on our platform.

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:05.639
Because when I
started two years ago,

00:06:05.639 --> 00:06:07.180
we didn't have a
ton of Ruby support.

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:09.220
And so seeing that
transformation over the time,

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:11.050
being able to talk
to my community

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:13.720
at events like RubyConf about
the cool stuff we're doing.

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:15.303
It's been really
exciting, and they're

00:06:15.303 --> 00:06:16.600
excited to hear it, too.

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:16.930
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:18.070
So I guess that
means, yeah, so you've

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:20.528
got the support there for the
frameworks and the tools that

00:06:20.528 --> 00:06:22.990
come into the Ruby community
to be to deploy your stuff, as

00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:25.510
well as all the SDKs, and
tools, and gems, I guess,

00:06:25.510 --> 00:06:29.100
to interact with all our
backend services, like storage

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:30.860
and all our other things.

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:32.860
AJA HAMMERLY: Yeah, I'm
actually really excited.

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:34.776
We're doing a bunch of
examples work right now

00:06:34.776 --> 00:06:37.290
and flushing out the MLAPIs.

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:39.095
I did a cool talk at
RailsConf for using

00:06:39.095 --> 00:06:42.010
the Vision API to do kind
of a "Where in the World

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:42.760
is Carmen Diego."

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:45.160
I think that might have been
your idea, Mark, that I stole.

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:46.137
MARK MANDEL: That is
a pretty awesome idea.

00:06:46.137 --> 00:06:47.170
I have to be honest with you.

00:06:47.170 --> 00:06:48.130
AJA HAMMERLY: So we
use the Vision API

00:06:48.130 --> 00:06:49.330
to recognize landmarks.

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:53.186
And we used it to
recognize-- and we

00:06:53.186 --> 00:06:55.600
did text-- speech and text
we translate with a sign that

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:57.070
was in Japanese.

00:06:57.070 --> 00:07:00.130
We used it to recognize
various other things.

00:07:00.130 --> 00:07:02.707
We did some geocoding based
on where landmarks were.

00:07:02.707 --> 00:07:03.790
And we did it all in Ruby.

00:07:03.790 --> 00:07:05.297
And it was really fun.

00:07:05.297 --> 00:07:07.880
And the fact that I was able to
do that all in Ruby was great.

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:08.870
And the community
has been great.

00:07:08.870 --> 00:07:10.450
We've been getting community
contributions, back

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:12.134
to our examples
repo, which I'm super

00:07:12.134 --> 00:07:14.800
excited about because that means
the community wants to help us.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:16.600
And pull requests welcome.

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:18.850
So it's been fantastic.

00:07:18.850 --> 00:07:23.006
And we have support now
also for Visions Out.

00:07:23.006 --> 00:07:24.130
We're working on Translate.

00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:27.070
We have got support
for Speech coming out,

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:28.660
or in Flight at least.

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:30.160
And all this is
happening at GitHub.

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:32.260
You can easily see what's
working-- what's being

00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:33.760
worked on at any given time.

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:35.650
And it's been fun.

00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:38.920
And Ruby doesn't
have a ton of ML.

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:40.570
The Ruby community
and the ML community

00:07:40.570 --> 00:07:42.910
don't overlap a huge
amount, and so being

00:07:42.910 --> 00:07:44.770
able to bring these
tools in to these people

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:47.920
in a way that is really
simple to use has been great.

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:49.547
Because calling in
huge ML libraries,

00:07:49.547 --> 00:07:51.880
like C libraries and stuff,
and then trying to link them

00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:53.890
into Ruby is kind of a pain.

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:57.040
Whereas the cloud
ML libraries, it's

00:07:57.040 --> 00:07:59.660
just an API call and something
that we already know.

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:00.650
And that's been great.

00:08:00.650 --> 00:08:01.566
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:08:01.566 --> 00:08:04.900
So as you know,
I'm not a Rubyist.

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:06.790
So can you tell us
a little bit more

00:08:06.790 --> 00:08:11.980
about how is the experience, if
you were a Ruby developer that

00:08:11.980 --> 00:08:15.430
has done Ruby apps,
let's say web apps,

00:08:15.430 --> 00:08:17.260
on other platforms
or maybe locally,

00:08:17.260 --> 00:08:19.386
what is the experience of
moving that to the cloud?

00:08:19.386 --> 00:08:21.468
AJA HAMMERLY: So this is
the part I like the best.

00:08:21.468 --> 00:08:23.140
Once you figure out
the database piece,

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:25.360
and you can run your-- if
you're going to use Flex,

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:27.070
you can run your
database in Cloud SQL,

00:08:27.070 --> 00:08:29.420
you can run it as a
separate instance,

00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:30.836
you can use Datastore.

00:08:30.836 --> 00:08:32.919
Once you figure out the
database piece-- and doing

00:08:32.919 --> 00:08:34.150
that's actually pretty
straightforward-- you just

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:36.360
go in and edit the standard file
that you would edit to change

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:38.068
to your production
database configuration

00:08:38.068 --> 00:08:40.400
or just edit your production
database configuration,

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:42.590
you type "gcloud app deploy."

00:08:42.590 --> 00:08:43.360
And it goes.

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:44.110
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:08:44.110 --> 00:08:46.026
AJA HAMMERLY: And it
asks, is this a Ruby app?

00:08:46.026 --> 00:08:48.220
And you say, yes, this
is, in fact, a Ruby app.

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:48.910
And it goes.

00:08:48.910 --> 00:08:51.430
And the defaults work for
the vast, vast majority

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:53.320
of applications.

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:55.930
And the other nice thing is
that a lot of our libraries

00:08:55.930 --> 00:08:57.860
have been written by hand.

00:08:57.860 --> 00:09:00.220
And so there's a lot of stuff
that just feels natural.

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:02.290
My favorite example is
our BigQuery library.

00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:04.750
So BigQuery is the
database product.

00:09:04.750 --> 00:09:07.120
And if you go into co-create
a new table in BigQuery

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:09.850
using the Ruby
library, use a syntax

00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:12.190
that is nearly identical
to the syntax you use

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:13.550
to write migrations in Rails.

00:09:13.550 --> 00:09:15.025
It's the same kind
of block format.

00:09:15.025 --> 00:09:17.400
And the first time I did it,
I'm like, wait, I know this.

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:20.109
It was that moment from
"Jurassic Park" again.

00:09:20.109 --> 00:09:21.400
But we do that in other places.

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:23.609
The Vision API, one of
the kind of things in Ruby

00:09:23.609 --> 00:09:25.650
is there's always more
than one way to do things.

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:27.700
It's kind of
anti-Python in that way.

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:30.850
Although the communities are
largely overlapping and syntax

00:09:30.850 --> 00:09:32.470
looks similar a lot of the time.

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:34.780
And the Vision API has four
or five different methods

00:09:34.780 --> 00:09:37.420
to send out a request, even
though there's only actually

00:09:37.420 --> 00:09:39.891
one vision Endpoint on
the API on Google's side.

00:09:39.891 --> 00:09:41.890
And that just feels natural
because I can ask it

00:09:41.890 --> 00:09:43.720
for landmarks, or I
can ask it for faces,

00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:45.790
or I can ask it for labels.

00:09:45.790 --> 00:09:47.370
And it's sending a request.

00:09:47.370 --> 00:09:49.870
It's the same request out, but
I can actually get the data

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:51.470
that I want in that situation.

00:09:51.470 --> 00:09:52.720
So it's been fun.

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:54.428
FRANCESC CAMPOY: If
I remember correctly,

00:09:54.428 --> 00:09:56.050
you made a video
not that long ago

00:09:56.050 --> 00:09:59.579
about how to make
BigQuery tables with Ruby.

00:09:59.579 --> 00:10:00.370
AJA HAMMERLY: Yeah.

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:02.064
I've been working on
a new video series

00:10:02.064 --> 00:10:03.980
as part of our Cloud
Minute that's using Ruby.

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:06.280
We did "Making a
BigQuery table."

00:10:06.280 --> 00:10:09.880
We did "Uploading a
Picture to Cloud Storage."

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:11.954
And I used a lovely
picture of a mountain goat

00:10:11.954 --> 00:10:13.120
from a hike I took recently.

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:14.770
It was fantastic.

00:10:14.770 --> 00:10:16.780
And we've also been doing
some videos with some

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:18.040
of the community libraries.

00:10:18.040 --> 00:10:22.750
Fog is the multi-cloud
provisioning and deployment

00:10:22.750 --> 00:10:24.720
type library for Ruby.

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:25.720
It supports AWS.

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:26.730
It supports Rackspace.

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:28.450
It supports a lot of stuff.

00:10:28.450 --> 00:10:30.430
And there's several
folks at Google now

00:10:30.430 --> 00:10:33.050
who are working on
improving fog-google.

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:35.290
And I've been doing demos
with fog-google showing how

00:10:35.290 --> 00:10:38.011
you can use the tools that
you're probably already using

00:10:38.011 --> 00:10:39.760
to interact with our
platform, in addition

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:40.960
to the platforms
you might be using

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:42.490
or the stuff you might
be more familiar with.

00:10:42.490 --> 00:10:43.600
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:45.490
I think that there's a question.

00:10:45.490 --> 00:10:47.050
We went over it real quick.

00:10:47.050 --> 00:10:51.790
You mentioned the
Datastore or Cloud SQL.

00:10:51.790 --> 00:10:54.370
In case people don't know,
what is the difference?

00:10:54.370 --> 00:10:55.795
Could we talk a
little bit about,

00:10:55.795 --> 00:10:58.870
in terms of a Rubyist,
what is Datastore?

00:10:58.870 --> 00:11:02.980
AJA HAMMERLY: So Datastore is
our NoSQL database in Google,

00:11:02.980 --> 00:11:03.520
at Google.

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:05.650
And to be honest, I
don't know a ton of folks

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:07.630
who are using NoSQL with Rails.

00:11:07.630 --> 00:11:08.680
I'm sure there are some.

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:10.630
In fact, we talked about that a
couple of minutes ago before we

00:11:10.630 --> 00:11:11.650
started.

00:11:11.650 --> 00:11:14.590
Several of us have done
that at various points.

00:11:14.590 --> 00:11:18.100
But a Cloud SQL is
our hosted MySQL,

00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:20.290
and it's just a
drop-in replacement.

00:11:20.290 --> 00:11:22.990
You can use the exact same
MySQL gem that you use.

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:26.050
And you just point it
at the correct host IP

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:27.730
that we give you,
and it just works.

00:11:27.730 --> 00:11:29.320
It's great.

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:32.220
And then if you want, a lot
of Rubyists like PostgreSQL.

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:33.120
I like PostgreSQL.

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:34.050
And if you want
to run PostgreSQL,

00:11:34.050 --> 00:11:36.480
you can totally run PostgreSQL
on a Google Compute Engine

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:38.590
instance and manage your
own PostgreSQL instance,

00:11:38.590 --> 00:11:39.520
if you want.

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:42.146
MARK MANDEL: I know we also
have Deployment Manager

00:11:42.146 --> 00:11:43.270
through the Cloud Launcher.

00:11:43.270 --> 00:11:44.280
You can deploy
PostgreSQL that way,

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:45.420
and it works out quite nicely.

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:45.690
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:48.220
So you basically-- you just
click once, deploy it, that's

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:48.720
it.

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:49.375
MARK MANDEL: That's it.

00:11:49.375 --> 00:11:49.909
Nice.

00:11:49.909 --> 00:11:52.200
So I know you do a lot of
stuff on the monitoring side.

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:54.930
Are there any hooks there
for monitoring Rails apps

00:11:54.930 --> 00:11:56.230
or Ruby apps as well?

00:11:56.230 --> 00:11:57.090
AJA HAMMERLY: So
we've got a team.

00:11:57.090 --> 00:11:59.381
We've actually built a really
fun Ruby team in Seattle.

00:11:59.381 --> 00:12:01.380
There's four or five
of us who work on Ruby

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:03.480
on a regular basis,
including several doves who

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:04.590
are working full-time now.

00:12:04.590 --> 00:12:06.048
And one of the
things they're doing

00:12:06.048 --> 00:12:07.830
is they're making
Stackdriver integration

00:12:07.830 --> 00:12:10.132
libraries for Rails and Ruby.

00:12:10.132 --> 00:12:12.090
I believe the login
library went out last week,

00:12:12.090 --> 00:12:13.830
and the rest of them
are in progress.

00:12:13.830 --> 00:12:16.560
And this is all, again, on
GitHub under the Google Cloud

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:19.494
Platform "G Cloud Ruby Project."

00:12:19.494 --> 00:12:20.660
So you can see our progress.

00:12:20.660 --> 00:12:23.020
You can see what issues
have been entered.

00:12:23.020 --> 00:12:25.020
Obviously, we're going
to have some integrations

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:27.810
with other parts of the system,
GRPC libraries, and things

00:12:27.810 --> 00:12:28.350
like that.

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:30.150
And so if there's particular
issues you're running into,

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:31.983
you can see if someone
else has reported it,

00:12:31.983 --> 00:12:33.670
and how close we
are to fixing it.

00:12:33.670 --> 00:12:34.630
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So you
mentioned a little bit

00:12:34.630 --> 00:12:36.463
about the things that
you've been working on

00:12:36.463 --> 00:12:39.450
and what's coming up real soon.

00:12:39.450 --> 00:12:40.740
What else is coming up?

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:44.035
What are you excited about
and that other Rubyists might

00:12:44.035 --> 00:12:45.910
be excited about it on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:12:45.910 --> 00:12:48.510
AJA HAMMERLY: So I'm actually
really excited about the fact

00:12:48.510 --> 00:12:51.420
that we just recently had
the App Engine Flex beta 2,

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:54.510
and that App Engine Flex is
getting very, very stable

00:12:54.510 --> 00:12:57.270
and starting to
look really good.

00:12:57.270 --> 00:13:00.120
In Ruby, we have some
platform-as-a-service options,

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:02.580
but always having more is great.

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:04.440
As I say pretty
frequently when people

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:07.020
ask me to compare
various providers,

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:09.390
they're all different, but
competition helps everyone.

00:13:09.390 --> 00:13:10.770
Because everyone's
pushing the envelope,

00:13:10.770 --> 00:13:12.478
making the experience
better, and setting

00:13:12.478 --> 00:13:15.990
a higher bar for
how developers get

00:13:15.990 --> 00:13:17.760
to interact with these tools.

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:20.040
So I'm really excited
about App Engine Flex.

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:21.420
I'm also excited
that we're doing

00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:25.280
some automatic code-generated
libraries for some of the APIs

00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:27.780
that we haven't gotten around
to yet-- haven't gotten around

00:13:27.780 --> 00:13:29.209
to doing the hand-coding on.

00:13:29.209 --> 00:13:31.500
And those are happening--
I'm happy those are happening

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:33.416
because that means there's
more and more stuff

00:13:33.416 --> 00:13:35.550
that I can just
interact with from Ruby.

00:13:35.550 --> 00:13:38.610
I don't have to worry
about creating my own HTTP

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:41.520
request to hit the REST
endpoints, for example.

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:43.290
And I'm also just
happy that we're

00:13:43.290 --> 00:13:45.040
coming out with more examples.

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:47.490
The team here is working
on lots of cool tutorials

00:13:47.490 --> 00:13:48.820
on how to hook pieces together.

00:13:48.820 --> 00:13:49.819
We're doing more videos.

00:13:49.819 --> 00:13:51.750
We're doing blog posts.

00:13:51.750 --> 00:13:54.419
We had a post on the open
source blog earlier this year.

00:13:54.419 --> 00:13:56.460
And we're just trying to
get more stuff out there

00:13:56.460 --> 00:13:57.570
to get part of the community.

00:13:57.570 --> 00:13:59.100
And I'm really excited
that as a Rubyist,

00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:01.308
a longtime Rubyist, that I
can say, hey check it out.

00:14:01.308 --> 00:14:03.087
We've got stuff for you.

00:14:03.087 --> 00:14:04.920
And there's stuff coming
from the community.

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:06.961
A couple of months ago,
there was an announcement

00:14:06.961 --> 00:14:09.090
that someone had done
Ruby TensorFlow bindings.

00:14:09.090 --> 00:14:10.680
And that came completely
from the community,

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:12.205
and I was super
excited about that.

00:14:12.205 --> 00:14:13.120
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is really cool.

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:15.304
AJA HAMMERLY: And I love
my community so much,

00:14:15.304 --> 00:14:17.220
that I'm glad there's
stuff that they can use.

00:14:17.220 --> 00:14:19.260
I'm like, we have all these cool
things, and you can use them.

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:20.220
It's fantastic.

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:21.474
MARK MANDEL: That's great.

00:14:21.474 --> 00:14:23.640
You touched on, I think,
an interesting point there.

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:25.800
You're talking about
comparing providers.

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:27.630
I think the elephant
in the room a little,

00:14:27.630 --> 00:14:29.130
the traditional--
I guess that might

00:14:29.130 --> 00:14:31.770
be the right word-- people
tend to go towards Heroku.

00:14:31.770 --> 00:14:34.353
Why might they be interested in
going to Google Cloud Platform

00:14:34.353 --> 00:14:34.860
instead?

00:14:34.860 --> 00:14:36.720
AJA HAMMERLY: So yeah, I
don't know if traditional

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:37.511
is the right thing.

00:14:37.511 --> 00:14:39.630
But I work with a lot
of boot camp students,

00:14:39.630 --> 00:14:42.000
and a lot of them learn
Rails because it's

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:43.410
the
convention-over-configuration

00:14:43.410 --> 00:14:45.034
thing makes it easy
to learn for people

00:14:45.034 --> 00:14:46.700
who don't have a
ton of experience

00:14:46.700 --> 00:14:49.080
because there is a right way.

00:14:49.080 --> 00:14:52.650
And they often use Heroku
for their early deployments.

00:14:52.650 --> 00:14:53.400
So Heroku's great.

00:14:53.400 --> 00:14:54.810
I've got a lot of
friends who work there.

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:56.476
It's got a lot of
great things about it.

00:14:56.476 --> 00:14:59.250
I think that our App Engine
Flex and Heroku are slightly

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:01.795
different in some ways.

00:15:01.795 --> 00:15:04.170
App Engine Flex gives you a
lot of options about database

00:15:04.170 --> 00:15:05.260
layer, for example.

00:15:05.260 --> 00:15:08.100
You have to pick-- you have
to pick a database layer.

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:10.029
And it also is based
on container image.

00:15:10.029 --> 00:15:11.820
So you can actually
see the whole way down.

00:15:11.820 --> 00:15:13.650
The container image is
available to the public.

00:15:13.650 --> 00:15:15.649
You can see the whole way
down what we're doing.

00:15:15.649 --> 00:15:17.550
Now Heroku's got a
lot of integrations.

00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:19.470
They've been around
for a long time.

00:15:19.470 --> 00:15:21.270
So there's a lot of people
who like their integrations.

00:15:21.270 --> 00:15:23.686
But we also have integrations
with things like Stackdriver

00:15:23.686 --> 00:15:25.050
to do the monitoring.

00:15:25.050 --> 00:15:27.030
And we have integrations
with our MLAPIs.

00:15:27.030 --> 00:15:30.150
We have integrations
with Datastore,

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:32.670
for example, if you
want a NoSQL solution.

00:15:32.670 --> 00:15:35.125
And so I think it's-- I always
say this when people ask me

00:15:35.125 --> 00:15:36.000
to compare providers.

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:37.200
I get asked a lot.

00:15:37.200 --> 00:15:39.120
It really depends on
your use case and stuff,

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:40.619
but you can generally
figure it out.

00:15:40.619 --> 00:15:42.310
Also our pricing
model is different,

00:15:42.310 --> 00:15:44.370
and so that's another thing
that people should consider,

00:15:44.370 --> 00:15:45.600
is looking at how
the pricing differs

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:48.080
based on what they think
their use case is going to be.

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:49.350
MARK MANDEL: I know
you were talking about,

00:15:49.350 --> 00:15:51.690
when we were talking before
we started recording, about

00:15:51.690 --> 00:15:53.820
basically the flexibility
that having everything running

00:15:53.820 --> 00:15:55.380
on Docker containers
also gives you.

00:15:55.380 --> 00:15:56.460
AJA HAMMERLY: Yeah, so
it's the other thing

00:15:56.460 --> 00:15:57.460
is I like to talk about.

00:15:57.460 --> 00:16:00.300
And I have a talk half-written
but haven't gotten an accepted

00:16:00.300 --> 00:16:00.960
anywhere.

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:04.800
It's "Growing Your Application
on Google Cloud Platform."

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:07.590
And you start with a bare
minimum viable deployment.

00:16:07.590 --> 00:16:09.150
You do it on Flex
because it's easy,

00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:10.740
and you can iterate as
quickly as you need to.

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:12.090
And you don't have to
worry about the scaling

00:16:12.090 --> 00:16:13.757
because we'll take
care of that for you.

00:16:13.757 --> 00:16:15.298
But then you realize
you want to move

00:16:15.298 --> 00:16:17.400
to a microservices
architecture, or perhaps you

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:19.830
want to do something
interesting with your database,

00:16:19.830 --> 00:16:21.150
and so you need to
move part of it off.

00:16:21.150 --> 00:16:22.320
And so perhaps you
move parts of it

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:24.696
to Kubernetes because you have
the container image there,

00:16:24.696 --> 00:16:26.445
and you can take it,
and you can strip out

00:16:26.445 --> 00:16:28.761
the stuff you don't want
from our base container image

00:16:28.761 --> 00:16:29.760
and then build your own.

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:31.885
Or you can look at how we
did it and build your own

00:16:31.885 --> 00:16:32.562
based on that.

00:16:32.562 --> 00:16:34.020
And then eventually,
maybe, there's

00:16:34.020 --> 00:16:36.502
pieces that you just really
need to do on a plain old VM.

00:16:36.502 --> 00:16:38.460
Maybe you're doing some
crazy image processing,

00:16:38.460 --> 00:16:40.085
or video transcoding,
or something else

00:16:40.085 --> 00:16:41.700
that's super compute intensive.

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:44.340
And it just makes more sense
cost-wise or performance-wise

00:16:44.340 --> 00:16:46.832
for you to do it straight
up on Google Compute Engine.

00:16:46.832 --> 00:16:48.540
And since you're
already on our platform,

00:16:48.540 --> 00:16:50.640
your data's already there,
you've got the project set up,

00:16:50.640 --> 00:16:52.290
you've got the
permissions set up,

00:16:52.290 --> 00:16:55.140
it's really easy to grow
as your application grows

00:16:55.140 --> 00:16:57.780
on our platform in
whatever way you need to.

00:16:57.780 --> 00:17:00.660
And I think that's a
huge advantage of using

00:17:00.660 --> 00:17:03.230
a platform like ours where
there's so many products

00:17:03.230 --> 00:17:05.480
and so many ways to do things,
is that we can meet you

00:17:05.480 --> 00:17:08.670
or you're at, wherever you
are in your product lifecycle.

00:17:08.670 --> 00:17:10.829
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So we
interviewed, some time ago,

00:17:10.829 --> 00:17:14.760
Russel Smith from
Rainforest QA, and I

00:17:14.760 --> 00:17:17.589
think there was this
interesting thing.

00:17:17.589 --> 00:17:22.800
So Rainforest QA runs a
lot of things on Heroku.

00:17:22.800 --> 00:17:27.599
But they also access BigQuery
and other technologies

00:17:27.599 --> 00:17:28.710
that are on Google Cloud.

00:17:28.710 --> 00:17:30.870
I think that that's also
pretty interesting things.

00:17:30.870 --> 00:17:32.640
Even if you are
running on Heroku,

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:36.580
can you use things like,
I don't know, Stackdriver?

00:17:37.455 --> 00:17:39.330
AJA HAMMERLY: Stackdriver
doesn't necessarily

00:17:39.330 --> 00:17:41.621
make as much sense because
Heroku's a managed solution,

00:17:41.621 --> 00:17:46.110
but you could definitely
use our MLAPIs very easily.

00:17:46.110 --> 00:17:47.610
BigQuery is actually
really popular.

00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:49.068
It's one of the
things that I think

00:17:49.068 --> 00:17:51.870
got the fastest adoption
in the Ruby community.

00:17:51.870 --> 00:17:54.540
And I know a number
of Ruby companies

00:17:54.540 --> 00:17:56.070
that run Rails
that use BigQuery.

00:17:56.070 --> 00:17:57.919
And you can totally
use that from anywhere.

00:17:57.919 --> 00:17:59.460
You just have to
put your data in it.

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:01.110
And streaming it, it's a gem.

00:18:01.110 --> 00:18:03.060
You install it
like any other gem.

00:18:03.060 --> 00:18:05.310
You create the object,
and you send stuff to it.

00:18:05.310 --> 00:18:06.300
It's fantastic.

00:18:06.300 --> 00:18:08.550
It's two or three lines of code.

00:18:08.550 --> 00:18:09.360
I love simple.

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:10.630
I love easy.

00:18:10.630 --> 00:18:13.170
And so, yeah, even if you're
running in another place,

00:18:13.170 --> 00:18:14.850
you can still use our tools
where they make sense.

00:18:14.850 --> 00:18:16.290
If you're running
on AWS, you can

00:18:16.290 --> 00:18:19.600
use all the Stackdriver
tools because Stackdriver's

00:18:19.600 --> 00:18:20.790
across cloud.

00:18:20.790 --> 00:18:23.040
And use the pieces
that make sense.

00:18:23.040 --> 00:18:24.840
We've got a huge platform.

00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:27.960
Find the pieces that make sense
for you based on your workload.

