00:22:31.740 --> 00:22:35.970
And now we are going to
talk about Kubernetes.

00:22:35.970 --> 00:22:38.760
We have our request of the week.

00:22:38.760 --> 00:22:43.860
OK, so imagine I have a
heterogeneous cluster,

00:22:43.860 --> 00:22:46.855
so not all dimensions
are exactly the same.

00:22:46.855 --> 00:22:48.480
Not all the nodes
are exactly the same.

00:22:48.480 --> 00:22:48.810
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:22:48.810 --> 00:22:50.893
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And let's
say that, for instance,

00:22:50.893 --> 00:22:53.304
some of the nodes have
SSD and the others do not.

00:22:53.304 --> 00:22:53.970
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:22:53.970 --> 00:22:56.344
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And I want
to run some of the containers

00:22:56.344 --> 00:23:00.780
that require, or that run
better, let's say, on SSDs.

00:23:00.780 --> 00:23:04.080
So you want to say, hey,
my cluster is very big

00:23:04.080 --> 00:23:06.720
and have a lot of those nodes,
but whenever it is possible,

00:23:06.720 --> 00:23:12.402
run these kind of pods
on these kind of nodes.

00:23:12.402 --> 00:23:14.610
MARK MANDEL: So something
that's very Disk I/O heavy,

00:23:14.610 --> 00:23:16.090
maybe is doing some
data processing,

00:23:16.090 --> 00:23:18.040
maybe you're running a
database, something like that.

00:23:18.040 --> 00:23:19.665
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Let's imagine that you

00:23:19.665 --> 00:23:21.359
have a GPUs, for instance.

00:23:21.359 --> 00:23:23.400
You have GPUs, and you
want to do some rendering,

00:23:23.400 --> 00:23:26.040
so you want to make sure that
the container is running--

00:23:26.040 --> 00:23:28.390
rendering run on
a node with a GPU.

00:23:28.390 --> 00:23:29.640
MARK MANDEL: That makes sense.

00:23:29.640 --> 00:23:32.175
FRANCESC CAMPOY: How would
you do that with Kubernetes?

00:23:32.175 --> 00:23:34.620
MARK MANDEL: So
that's pretty cool.

00:23:34.620 --> 00:23:36.786
So if anyone's done
anything with Kubernetes,

00:23:36.786 --> 00:23:38.160
you're probably
familiar with how

00:23:38.160 --> 00:23:42.450
pods have labels and services
built on top of those to say,

00:23:42.450 --> 00:23:45.450
OK, give me a selector that I'm
going to-- so with a service,

00:23:45.450 --> 00:23:47.080
I'm going to have a
selector that says,

00:23:47.080 --> 00:23:50.565
I want to expose all the pods
that have these labels, right?

00:23:50.565 --> 00:23:52.440
So you've got these
arbitrary key value pairs

00:23:52.440 --> 00:23:53.790
that you can attach.

00:23:53.790 --> 00:23:56.700
So Kubernetes kind of extends
that whole idea of key value

00:23:56.700 --> 00:23:59.370
pairs with labels, but rather
than having them on the pods,

00:23:59.370 --> 00:24:01.102
you're able to add
labels to nodes.

00:24:01.102 --> 00:24:02.310
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Interesting.

00:24:02.310 --> 00:24:06.690
MARK MANDEL: So you could
have type GPU or type SSD

00:24:06.690 --> 00:24:08.140
on nodes within your cluster.

00:24:08.140 --> 00:24:10.532
And you can add
those as you see fit.

00:24:10.532 --> 00:24:12.240
What's neat, then, is
you have this thing

00:24:12.240 --> 00:24:15.900
where you can have a node
selector in part of your pod

00:24:15.900 --> 00:24:19.470
specification, where you do your
deployment or your replica set.

00:24:19.470 --> 00:24:20.970
And you can add a
node selector that

00:24:20.970 --> 00:24:25.080
says, hey, I want these only
to run on these nodes that

00:24:25.080 --> 00:24:28.044
have these key value pairs,
these labels, till you

00:24:28.044 --> 00:24:28.710
have a selector.

00:24:28.710 --> 00:24:30.290
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:24:30.290 --> 00:24:31.980
I'm looking at the
documentation now.

00:24:31.980 --> 00:24:33.730
It actually looks pretty simple.

00:24:33.730 --> 00:24:36.870
It's basically saying,
I just want these.

00:24:36.870 --> 00:24:40.170
So it really, in the metadata of
the pod, when you're describing

00:24:40.170 --> 00:24:42.120
it, where you're going
to say something like,

00:24:42.120 --> 00:24:43.980
I want the node
affinity is going

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:47.670
to be something like, run
this on something that

00:24:47.670 --> 00:24:48.686
has these values.

00:24:48.686 --> 00:24:49.435
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:24:49.435 --> 00:24:51.985
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So in
our case, in our node

00:24:51.985 --> 00:24:56.110
that has one of the labels
is something like GPU.

00:24:56.110 --> 00:24:58.110
MARK MANDEL: GPU,
something like that.

00:24:58.110 --> 00:24:59.730
You said an interesting
word as well.

00:24:59.730 --> 00:25:03.100
The basic version of node
selector is very simple.

00:25:03.100 --> 00:25:07.685
It's just this
combination of labels.

00:25:07.685 --> 00:25:08.560
And it's very simple.

00:25:08.560 --> 00:25:11.540
It's just an or-- it's
just an and Boolean.

00:25:11.540 --> 00:25:13.290
But there is an alpha
feature of affinity,

00:25:13.290 --> 00:25:15.873
which you said before, and also
anti-affinity, which gives you

00:25:15.873 --> 00:25:20.280
a lot more control and a lot
more complex label selection

00:25:20.280 --> 00:25:21.310
capabilities as well.

00:25:21.310 --> 00:25:24.487
So you can do affinity, like
this running on these machines.

00:25:24.487 --> 00:25:26.070
You can do preferred
affinity, like it

00:25:26.070 --> 00:25:28.769
would be really great if it did,
but if it doesn't, that's fine.

00:25:28.769 --> 00:25:30.810
And then you can do the
anti version of that too.

00:25:30.810 --> 00:25:32.280
So I don't want
these running here.

00:25:32.280 --> 00:25:33.955
Don't put on these machines.

00:25:33.955 --> 00:25:35.430
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Or I'd rather not

00:25:35.430 --> 00:25:36.490
have [INAUDIBLE]
on these machines,

00:25:36.490 --> 00:25:38.430
but if there's any
other choice-- whatever.

00:25:38.430 --> 00:25:39.806
MARK MANDEL: Exactly.

00:25:39.806 --> 00:25:40.930
So that's an alpha feature.

00:25:40.930 --> 00:25:42.179
It's coming down the pipeline.

00:25:42.179 --> 00:25:44.400
But it looks like, yeah,
you've got a lot of control,

00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:46.566
especially if you're running
heterogeneous clusters.

00:25:46.566 --> 00:25:48.134
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:25:48.134 --> 00:25:49.800
Well, I think that
answers the question.

